INT. GRAIN SILO - BASE LEVEL - NIGHT

A concrete cylinder, ninety feet of dark above. Dust hangs in the flashlight beams. A rusted auger runs up the wall like a spine. Underfoot, a drift of old grain crunches.

FOUR TEENAGERS stand in a loose ring: DEVAKI RAMANATHAN, 17, in a puffer jacket, a camcorder on her shoulder; her brother HARISH, 15, with a flashlight in each fist; MARGUERITE "MAGPIE" LONGSTREET, 16, in a leather jacket with a patch on the sleeve; and THADDEUS BELL, 17, in a school letterman's jacket, shivering.

HARISH
(to the group)
Okay. We're in. Can we go now?

MAGPIE
We just got here.

THADDEUS
There's a rule. You stay until midnight.

DEVAKI
(muttering)
There's no rule.

MAGPIE
There's always a rule.

A noise comes from above: a slow, scraping drag, like something heavy pulled across a floor. The four of them tilt their heads back together, flashlights swinging up to catch only dust.

HARISH
It's the wind.

THADDEUS
There's no wind.

DEVAKI
(quietly, into the camcorder)
Note the time. Eleven forty-one. Strange sounds in the silo.

The scraping stops. The silence that follows is worse. Thaddeus laughs, brittle. Magpie takes a step toward the ladder at the wall, puts a hand on the cold rung, and the whole silo hums with a faint metallic ring.

MAGPIE
Don't touch the ladder.

DEVAKI
I'm not touching it.

MAGPIE
I was telling myself.

Dust sifts down from above. Harish switches off one flashlight. Thaddeus scoots closer to Devaki. She keeps the camera trained on the dark, steady, her breath fogging. Magpie stays at the ladder, jaw clenched. Harish clicks the second flashlight on and off, on and off.

HARISH
(whispering)
Stop it.

DEVAKI
I'm not doing anything.

HARISH
Not you. Him.

He points the flashlight at Thaddeus. Thaddeus is looking up, mouth open, face washed white, and he points too.

Above them, high on the curved wall, something shifts. A long pale shape. Folded limbs, a head tilted wrong, hanging in the shadow. It does not move again.

THADDEUS
(a whisper)
That's a person.

MAGPIE
That's not a person.

DEVAKI
(into the camcorder)
It's not moving.

HARISH
Everyone, back to the door. Slowly.

They begin to back away, all four, hands out, flashlights trembling, the grain crunching underfoot with each step. Devaki keeps filming, walking backwards, and trips. The camcorder falls. The light on it swings wildly across the wall.

For one half-second it catches the shape. It is not hanging. It is crouching. Its head has turned.

THADDEUS
Run.

They scatter.
